[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". The reports say otherwise. The desk
merged them on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and
the same subjects cover two different causes. A parser fix will close only the first.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The two problems

1. **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, so tenants that write the day
   first get dates flipped. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: **fixed** by
   `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Ticket (closed, completed):
   [issue 1](https://github.com/ghostxxpol/what-it-shows-4471/issues/1).
2. **`hint`**: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants
   type what the hint says and the form reads it their way. The parser fix does not touch it.
   Engineering closed it as **won't fix**: the booking-v3 form is retired. Ticket (closed, not
   planned): [issue 2](https://github.com/ghostxxpol/what-it-shows-4471/issues/2).

<Callout kind="warn">
The intake tag is a guess made from the reporter's browser. It does not decide the problem. The
tenant does: its locale, and the booking form it runs.
</Callout>

## How the reports were placed

- One id, one report: the export repeats some rows, so each id is counted once.
- A report is `parser` or `hint` from its tenant, as `causes.ts` computes it. A `form` report from
  a booking-v3 tenant is `hint`. The rest are `parser`.
- Five `parser` reports read as a one-day move, not a swap (desk note `oneDayMove`). They stay
  counted under `parser`, footnoted by the table above. A dd/mm fix swaps month and day. It does
  not move a date by one day, so these five may still be broken. They may hide a time-zone bug.
  Nothing in the export proves it. They stay open in
  [issue 3](https://github.com/ghostxxpol/what-it-shows-4471/issues/3).

## What to change

- Split #4471 by cause, as above, and keep the five one-day reports open.
- Write a new decision record that replaces 0001: merge on cause, not on subject. Do not edit 0001.

